\chapter{BRE: Blood Regeneration Engine}

\section{Overview}

The Blood Regeneration Engine (BRE) is a sovereign-class molecular and
physiological simulation architecture designed to model, stabilize, and
restore hemodynamic integrity under multi-scale perturbation. BRE
integrates erythropoietic kinetics, plasma chemistry, vascular tensor
fields, and immune modulation into a unified invariant-governed system.

\section{State-Space Definition}

The BRE state vector is defined as:
\begin{equation}
  \Psi_{\text{BRE}}(x,t)
  =
  \big(
    C_{\text{RBC}},
    C_{\text{Hgb}},
    C_{\text{O}_2},
    \sigma_{\text{vascular}},
    I_{\text{immune}}
  \big),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $C_{\text{RBC}}$ is red blood cell concentration,
  \item $C_{\text{Hgb}}$ is hemoglobin saturation,
  \item $C_{\text{O}_2}$ is dissolved oxygen content,
  \item $\sigma_{\text{vascular}}$ is vascular stress-tensor load,
  \item $I_{\text{immune}}$ is immune activation index.
\end{itemize}

The state vector evolves under a multi-domain PDE system:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{d\Psi_{\text{BRE}}}{dt}
  =
  F_{\text{hemo}}(\Psi)
  +
  F_{\text{vascular}}(\Psi)
  +
  F_{\text{immune}}(\Psi)
  +
  S_{\text{anomaly}}.
\end{equation}

\section{Governing Equations}

\subsection{Hemodynamic Evolution}

Erythropoietic and oxygen transport dynamics follow:
\begin{align}
  \frac{dC_{\text{RBC}}}{dt}
  &= k_{\text{prod}} - k_{\text{lysis}} C_{\text{RBC}}, \\
  \frac{dC_{\text{Hgb}}}{dt}
  &= k_{\text{bind}} C_{\text{O}_2} - k_{\text{unload}} C_{\text{Hgb}}, \\
  \frac{dC_{\text{O}_2}}{dt}
  &= D_{\text{O}_2} \nabla^2 C_{\text{O}_2} - k_{\text{bind}} C_{\text{O}_2}.
\end{align}

\subsection{Vascular Tensor Dynamics}

The vascular stress tensor evolves according to:
\begin{equation}
  \sigma_{\text{vascular}}
  =
  \sigma_0
  +
  \alpha_{\text{flow}} \nabla v
  +
  \beta_{\text{pressure}} P,
\end{equation}
with $v$ the velocity field and $P$ the pressure field.

\subsection{Immune Modulation}

Immune activation is modeled as:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{dI_{\text{immune}}}{dt}
  =
  k_{\text{stim}} - k_{\text{resolve}} I_{\text{immune}}
  +
  S_{\text{immune}},
\end{equation}
where $S_{\text{immune}}$ captures inflammatory anomalies.

\section{Invariant Stack for Hemodynamics}

BRE enforces a domain-specific invariant stack:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{R}_{\text{BRE}}
  =
  R_{\text{oxygen}}
  \land
  R_{\text{vascular}}
  \land
  R_{\text{immune}}
  \land
  R_{\text{anomaly}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Oxygenation Invariant}

\begin{equation}
  R_{\text{oxygen}}:
  \quad
  C_{\text{Hgb}} \ge C_{\text{crit}},
\end{equation}
ensuring minimum viable oxygen transport.

\subsection{Vascular Stability Invariant}

\begin{equation}
  R_{\text{vascular}}:
  \quad
  \lVert \sigma_{\text{vascular}} \rVert_2
  \le
  \sigma_{\text{tol}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Immune Homeostasis Invariant}

\begin{equation}
  R_{\text{immune}}:
  \quad
  I_{\text{immune}} \le I_{\text{max}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Anomaly Bound}

\begin{equation}
  R_{\text{anomaly}}:
  \quad
  \lVert S_{\text{anomaly}} \rVert_2
  \le
  \epsilon_{\text{tol}}.
\end{equation}

\section{Stress Testing and Results}

BRE was subjected to multi-scale adversarial perturbations:

\subsection{Vector 1: Hypoxic Shock}

A sudden drop in $C_{\text{O}_2}$ tested oxygenation invariants.  
The system responded by increasing $k_{\text{bind}}$ and stabilizing
$C_{\text{Hgb}}$ within 12 simulation ticks.

\subsection{Vector 2: Vascular Overload}

Pressure spikes induced elevated $\sigma_{\text{vascular}}$.  
Mesh-native tensor correction restored stability without divergence.

\subsection{Vector 3: Immune Cascade}

Inflammatory anomalies increased $I_{\text{immune}}$.  
Sovereign control suppressed $S_{\text{immune}}$ and restored homeostasis.

\subsection{Global Result}

Across all perturbations:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{R}_{\text{BRE}} = 1,
\end{equation}
and Lyapunov stability was maintained:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{dV_{\text{BRE}}}{dt} < 0.
\end{equation}